    "description": "The Sock Shop microservices are all single-replica deployments, so each component is a single point of failure. The front-end is the only ingress, so killing it directly makes the store unavailable, violating steady state 1. The carts-db backend has no persistent storage, sets readOnlyRootFilesystem without a writable /data/db volume, and lacks probes; if it is killed, its pod may crash-loop or lose data, violating steady state 2. The sequence first applies StressChaos to front-end and carts-db to simulate heavy promotion traffic and database load, exposing resource limits and the lack of scaling/redundancy. Next, NetworkChaos injects packet loss/partition on the same pods to mimic a DDoS attack, which can cause liveness/readiness failures and force restarts. Then PodChaos pod-kill on front-end and carts-db simulates attackers deliberately killing critical pods, which is maximally impactful because there are no redundant replicas, and the carts-db restart reveals the read-only filesystem/persistence flaw that prevents recovery. Finally, PodChaos container-kill on the rabbitmq-exporter sidecar simulates a sidecar crash, which restarts the whole rabbitmq pod due to shared process group, disrupting order/queue processing. This sequence reveals the vulnerabilities of single replicas, storage misconfiguration, missing probes, and sidecar-caused pod restarts."
